      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGraw%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village was once known as \"Corset City\" during its 19th-century corset manufacturing boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGraw%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "McGraw, New York, was home to New-York Central College, the first college in the U.S. to accept all students, including women and African Americans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGraw%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Main Street Historic District and the Presbyterian Church of McGraw are listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGraw%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community was first settled in 1805 by Samuel McGraw, who built a log cabin near Trout Brook.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
